-- title:
--   Proof of Theorem 3, p. 5 — the quantity δ_k = (k − 1)[‖x_k − x*‖² − ‖x_{k−1} − x*‖²] + (α − 1)‖x_k − x*‖²
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal H$ be a real Hilbert space with norm $\|\cdot\|$, let $\alpha$ be a real parameter, let $(x_k)_{k\ge0}$ be a sequence in $\mathcal H$ and let $x^*\in\mathcal H$. In the proof of Theorem 3, Attouch and Peypouquet consider, for $k\ge1$, the quantity
--
--   $$\delta_k = (k-1)\big[\|x_k - x^*\|^2 - \|x_{k-1}-x^*\|^2\big] + (\alpha-1)\|x_k - x^*\|^2 .$$
--
--   With $h_k=\|x_k-x^*\|^2$ it reads $\delta_k=(k-1)(h_k-h_{k-1})+(\alpha-1)h_k$. It is used for a minimizer $x^*$ and a sequence generated by the accelerated forward-backward algorithm (2); its convergence yields the convergence of $\|z_k-x^*\|$, where $z_k=x_k+\frac{k-1}{\alpha-1}(x_k-x_{k-1})$ is the auxiliary sequence (6).
--
--   **Formalization Note** The objective $\Theta=\Psi+\Phi$, the extrapolated point $y_k$, algorithm (2) and the sequence $z_k$ of (6) are the shared definitions `theta`, `extrap`, `IsAccelFBRun` and `zSeq` of `NesterovFB.Rates.Algorithm`, which this file imports; only $\delta_k$ is defined here. In `x (k - 1)` the subtraction is on natural numbers; the theorems use $\delta_k$ only for $k\ge1$.
-- source:
--   Attouch and Peypouquet, The Rate of Convergence of Nesterov's Accelerated Forward-Backward Method is Actually Faster than 1/k^2, arXiv:1510.08740v4, p. 1, (1)-(2); p. 2, (6); p. 5, proof of Theorem 3 (definition of δ_k)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ThreeOpSplitting_ConvexRates_Problem
import Definitions.Def_NesterovFB_Rates_Algorithm

namespace NesterovFB.Weak

/-- The quantity of the proof of Theorem 3:
δ_k = (k − 1)(‖x_k − x*‖² − ‖x_{k−1} − x*‖²) + (α − 1)‖x_k − x*‖². -/
noncomputable def deltaSeq {H : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℝ H]
    (α : ℝ) (x : ℕ → H) (xstar : H) (k : ℕ) : ℝ :=
  ((k : ℝ) - 1) * (‖x k - xstar‖ ^ 2 - ‖x (k - 1) - xstar‖ ^ 2) + (α - 1) * ‖x k - xstar‖ ^ 2

end NesterovFB.Weak


